<Base lang="en" title="Kasganj Cleared 97.2% of eDistrict Applications in October 2026 — First in UP" description="In October 2026, Kasganj district cleared 97.20% of e-District applications and ranked first across Uttar Pradesh. Why the remaining 2.8% were left, what the application’s journey looks like, and how to keep your own application from being rejected." path="/en/blog/kasganj-certificate-clearance-october-2026/" altPath="/blog/kasganj-certificate-clearance-october-2026/" faqs={faqs} updated="6 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-06" dateModified="2026-11-06">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">Kasganj ranked first in UP in October 2026</h1>
<p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p>
<p class="updated-line">News of 5 November 2026 · Source given below</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/kasganj-certificate-clearance-october-2026-featured.webp" alt="Kasganj district topping Uttar Pradesh in eDistrict certificate clearance (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration • Illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>What happened</h2><p>In October 2026, Kasganj district cleared <strong>97.20% of e-District applications</strong> and ranked <strong>first across Uttar Pradesh</strong> — according to the report, the district was once again ahead of all others on this count. These are applications for income, caste, domicile and similar certificates filed through the eDistrict portal. More than <strong>36.90 crore certificates</strong> have been issued through eDistrict in Uttar Pradesh so far, and over 325 services from 47 departments are connected to it — so for one district to clear more than 97% is no small thing.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>What does 97.2% mean?</h2><p>This figure counts the applications processed in October 2026 — of all the applications disposed of in Kasganj that month, 97.20% were approved and certificates were issued. It does not mean that every application is made in a single day, nor that the remaining applications were rejected for good — some are still under verification, some have a shortcoming that can be fixed and resubmitted, and some are rejected. The clearance rate really shows how fast and clean a district’s system is running.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The application’s journey — from form to certificate</h2><p>Your application passes through several stages before the certificate is made, and a delay can happen at any of them. The journey usually looks like this:</p><ol><li><strong>Form and fee:</strong> Fill the form on eSathi, upload your documents and pay the ₹10–15 fee. You get an SRN number immediately — keep it safe.</li><li><strong>Field verification:</strong> The Lekhpal or Revenue Inspector checks your address and documents. This is usually the longest stage, especially when the workload is high.</li><li><strong>Tehsildar’s approval:</strong> After the verification report, the Tehsildar approves the application.</li><li><strong>Certificate issued:</strong> The digitally signed certificate is prepared — download it from the Citizen Login or find it in DigiLocker.</li></ol><p>Kasganj’s ranking shows that where field verification and approval happen on time, applications do not get delayed.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Why the remaining 2.8% were left</h2><p>Some applications get stuck in every district, and the reasons are usually the same ones written in our guides:</p><ul><li>A blurry scan or photo — if you cannot read it clearly yourself, the officer cannot either.</li><li>A difference between the name or date of birth and Aadhaar.</li><li>A missing self-declaration, or one in the wrong format.</li><li>A wrong rural/urban selection — this sends the verification to the wrong officer.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Why applications get rejected — 6 common reasons and their fixes</h2><p>Once you understand the reason for rejection, you will not repeat the mistake when you apply again:</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Blurry scan or photo</td><td>Scan in black &amp; white at 200 DPI, and open and read the file yourself before uploading</td></tr><tr><td>Name differs from Aadhaar</td><td>Write your name exactly as on your Aadhaar card — even a small difference becomes an obstacle</td></tr><tr><td>Missing self-declaration</td><td>Attach a self-declaration filled in the correct format and signed</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong rural/urban selection</td><td>Choose the correct option for your address — the Lekhpal verifies rural areas and the Revenue Inspector verifies urban areas</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong or old document</td><td>Check every document’s validity and format before uploading</td></tr><tr><td>Payment pending</td><td>Wait 24–48 hours after the payment is debited — do not pay again, and keep the transaction ID safe</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">Rejection does not mean a fine — you can fix the shortcoming and apply again, but first read the rejection remark.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>How to speed up your application in your district</h2><p>Kasganj’s example makes one thing clear — where the documents are complete from the start, the work moves fast. Prepare like this before you apply:</p><ul class="checklist"><li>Keep all documents ready in advance — Aadhaar, address proof, a photo and the self-declaration.</li><li>Keep every file below 100KB and open and check it yourself before uploading.</li><li>Keep the mobile number linked to Aadhaar active — the OTP comes to it.</li><li>Note down the SRN number as soon as you fill the form — the status is checked with it.</li><li>If a family member is available at the address during verification, the work moves faster.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>If your application is delayed</h2><p>First check the status on the official portal with your SRN number — the status can be seen even without logging in. Read the reason written in the remark. If the status is stuck on “Under Verification”, field verification may still be underway. After that, contact the helpdesk on <strong>0522-2304706</strong> or file a complaint on CM Helpline <strong>1076</strong>. And if the fixed time limit has passed and there is still no answer, there is also the appeal route under the <a href="/en/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate/">Janhit Guarantee Act 2011</a> — the first appeal goes to the SDM and the second to the DM.</p><figure class="shot"><img src="/images/status-page.webp" alt="eDistrict application status page (example)" /><figcaption>Example screenshot — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Where this news came from</h2><p>This information is from CertiSetu’s report of 5 November 2026, which cites Dainik Bhaskar, Jagran and ETV Bharat as sources. CertiSetu itself is a private platform, not a government website — so read this news in that light, and always treat the official portal’s information as final for your own application.</p></section>
